  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nWe will be in the midst of a pattern shift at the start of the long \nterm period (Wednesday) and there are some uncertainties worth \nnoting. Showers and isolated thunderstorms may be ongoing across \nparts of North GA on Wednesday morning. The location and extent of \nthis activity will be dependent on how things evolve on Tuesday and \nhow far the cold front makes it into the forecast area. \nAdditionally, we'll start to see surface high pressure extend \nsouthward from the NE US lending to a wedge front set up across the \nAppalachians beginning on Wednesday. Rain chances are likely to be \nhighest along and south of these respective boundaries where the \nwarm unstable airmass remains and/or where outflow boundaries from \nstorms on Tuesday evening end up. But again exact location remains \nuncertain at this time. Thus, rain and temperature forecast is lower \nconfidence for Wednesday across North GA. \n\nEnsembles and deterministic guidance continue to be in good \nagreement with strengthening of the wedge late Wednesday into \nThursday. A low pressure system traveling north off the Atlantic \nCoast line will help to reinforce high pressure with some models \nsuggesting a 1030-1035mb high setting up over the NE US and southern \nCanada. There may be just enough moisture to support isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms across North GA on Thursday but most look \nto remain dry. North to northeasterly low/mid-level flow will \nusher in drier air as we head into the latter half of the week and\nweekend which will be reflected in the temperature and \nprecipitation forecast. Forecast highs will be in the 70s/80s and \nforecast lows in the 50s/60s by Thursday with mostly dry weather \nFriday through early next week.",
